  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a CVT pulley change size?",
      "a": "Each pulley is two cones facing each other. Sliding the cones together pushes the belt outward to ride on a bigger circle; sliding them apart lets it sink to a smaller one.",
      "asks": [
        "How can a pulley get bigger?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the belt in a CVT made of?",
      "a": "Most car CVTs use a belt or chain made of many steel parts, running in special oil. Small scooters use a simpler rubber belt.",
      "asks": [
        "Is a CVT belt rubber?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the driven pulley?",
      "a": "The driven pulley is the one that turns towards the wheels. When the belt rides low on the drive pulley and high on the driven pulley, the wheels turn slowly with lots of force, like a low gear.",
      "asks": [
        "Which pulley turns the wheels?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What moves the pulley halves?",
      "a": "Oil pressure controlled by a computer slides one half of each pulley along its shaft. The two pulleys change size in opposite directions, so the belt stays tight.",
      "asks": [
        "What controls a CVT?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why can a CVT save fuel?",
      "a": "Because the ratio can be anything within its range, the CVT can hold the engine at the speed where it uses fuel most efficiently. The trade-off is a droning engine note when you accelerate hard.",
      "asks": [
        "Are CVTs efficient?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A CVT can give any ratio within its range, not just a set of fixed gears.",
    "The DAF 600 car of 1958 used an early rubber-belt CVT called the Variomatic.",
    "Many small scooters use a simple rubber-belt CVT that changes ratio automatically as the engine speeds up."
  ],
